---
title: Fried rice
description: Lesson 5.4. The module together, with ingredients you choose, and why yesterday's rice works.
servings: 2
time: 20 minutes
tags: [cooking-course, starch]
lesson: "5.4"
technique: stir-frying cooked rice
status: draft
---

> Technique: frying rice that was cooked the day before. Cooked starch
> changes as it chills, and that change is what lets the grains fry
> separately. The add-ins are slots: pick what you have and keep the
> amounts, about one part add-ins to two and a half parts rice.

Take @cooked rice{500%g}(cooked the day before, chilled; see the safety note) out of the fridge and break up the clumps with wet hands.

> Why day-old: the starch in cooked rice crystallises as it chills, and
> the grains turn firm enough to stand a second cooking. Freshly cooked
> rice forms soft, mushy clumps in the pan.
-- source: "How To Make Fried Rice without Starting a Day Ahead", America's Test Kitchen, 2023-09-10, https://www.americastestkitchen.com/articles/7660-make-fried-rice-without-starting-a-day-ahead

> Science: this is starch retrogradation. Cooking gelatinizes starch:
> the granules swell and lose their crystalline order. As the rice
> cools, the amylose and amylopectin chains line up and recrystallise,
> and water moves within the grain. The same process hardens cold rice
> and stales bread. Chilling drives it; freezing stops it.
> Look up: starch retrogradation, starch gelatinization, amylose,
> amylopectin.
-- source: Ning et al., "Starch retrogradation in starch-based foods: Mechanisms, influencing factors, and mitigation strategies", International Journal of Biological Macromolecules 300:140354, 2025, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ijbiomac.2025.140354 ; Bashir and Aggarwal, "Physicochemical, structural and functional properties of native and irradiated starch: a review", Journal of Food Science and Technology 56(2):513-523, 2019, https://doi.org/10.1007/s13197-018-3530-2 ; the America's Test Kitchen article above ("once rice freezes, retrogradation halts")

> Safety: uncooked rice can carry spores of Bacillus cereus, which
> survive cooking and can produce a toxin if cooked rice sits warm.
> Chill cooked rice as quickly as possible, ideally within 1 hour, by
> spreading it out; keep it in the fridge; eat it within 24 hours;
> reheat it until steaming hot all the way through, and only once.
-- source: Food Standards Scotland, "Food storage", https://www.foodstandards.gov.scot/consumers/food-safety/at-home/rice

Beat @eggs{2} with a pinch of salt. Heat a third of the @oil{3%tbsp}(sunflower or rapeseed) in a #large frying pan{} over high heat until it just starts to smoke. Pour in the eggs and stir for ~{30%seconds}, until just set. Tip them onto a plate.

> Why eggs first, and hot: they should be fully set before anything
> else goes in, and oil that is just starting to smoke cooks them
> fast.
-- source: Lan Lam, "A Love Letter to Simple Fried Rice", America's Test Kitchen, 2022-01-19, https://www.americastestkitchen.com/articles/4232-a-love-letter-to-simple-fried-rice

Add another third of the oil. Add @vegetables{100%g}(peas, carrot, pepper or sweetcorn, in small dice), @protein{100%g}(cooked ham, prawns, chicken or firm tofu, diced) and the white parts of @spring onions{3}(sliced, whites and greens kept apart). Stir-fry for ~{2%minutes}. Add @garlic{1%clove}(finely chopped) for the last few seconds.

Add the rest of the oil and the rice. Spread the rice over the pan, leave it for a minute, then toss, and repeat for ~{4%minutes}, until the grains are separate and hot through and some have toasted.

Pour @soy sauce{1.5%tbsp} around the edge of the pan. Return the eggs, breaking them up, add the green parts of the spring onions, and toss for ~{30%seconds}.

> If it goes wrong: rice that clumps and turns soft was too fresh. With
> no day-old rice, cook it with a little less water than usual, spread
> it on a tray for 10 minutes, then chill it for 20 before you fry.
-- source: the America's Test Kitchen article above (its full method also rinses the rice and coats it in oil before cooking)

> Next time, without the recipe: cold day-old rice, a very hot pan,
> eggs first and out, then add-ins in order of how long they take, the
> rice last, and the sauce at the end.
